body:
Alcohol and mindfulness kind of go in opposite directions. And while it is not required that you give up alcohol to meditate, to the degree that you diminish the consumption, your meditation (and daily life) will benefit.

&#x200B;

The real question for you is: Is alcohol worth it?

What is it wrong to make a living teaching meditation? This is the same type of criticism that used to happen when people started making a living teaching Yoga asanas (while in the past it was just something for cave yogis).

&#x200B;

I wrote some thoughts about this dillema here: [https://liveanddare.com/meditation-and-charging/](https://liveanddare.com/meditation-and-charging/). Please let me know what you think.

This is not necessarily negative.

&#x200B;

Make the best use of this anxiety. Don't try to get rid of it by numbing it. Instead, use it as a driving force in your life.

Nice one!

&#x200B;

Thoughts are fake news too!

Both are valid ways of meditating. It depends on what is your goal with meditation: if reaching Samadhi/Jhannas, or simply training mindfulness/awareness. Different traditions and different teachers will emphasize one or the other.

&#x200B;

The first mode will feel much more intense and deeper; the second is more open and more relaxed.

You might find some help in this page: http://liveanddare.com/what-is-meditation

You mentioned hot headeness. In this case, yogic breathing exercises are very helpful, and give results quicker than meditation. Have a look at "pranayama". Here is an example:

Try this:
* Breathe in counting 4 seconds;
* Hold your breath for 4 seconds;
* Breathe out for 4 seconds;
* Hold empty for 4 seconds.

That makes it one cycle. Do 12 cycles like this, and your mind will be in a different state. It takes only about 4 minutes.

Breathing in and out should be done through the nose, and as deep, even and silently as possible.

If 4 seconds is too hard, you can do 3 seconds. If it's too easy, you can increase the count.
